  "vulnerability_information": "MariaDB Connector/ODBC formats every diagnostic message with a prefix that embeds the server version string. The prefix is built in `MADB_PutErrorPrefix` (driver/ma_error.cpp:165) with the driver's custom `_snprintf`, which returns a NEGATIVE value when the output is truncated (driver/ma_platform_posix.cpp:41). The return value is added to `error->PrefixLen`, a `size_t` (unsigned, driver/ma_c_stuff.h:70). A malicious or MITM MariaDB server that sends a handshake `server_version` string longer than ~500 bytes causes `_snprintf` to truncate and return negative, wrapping `PrefixLen` to ~2^64. Every subsequent use of `PrefixLen` as an offset into the fixed 513-byte `SqlErrorMsg` buffer then writes out of bounds at a server-chosen backward offset.\n\nThis is an unauthenticated, remotely triggered client-side memory corruption that fires as soon as the application allocates a statement on the poisoned connection. Confirmed end-to-end with ASAN against the real driver built from HEAD 4e175d9 (v3.2.10).\n\n### Root cause\n\n1. Custom `_snprintf` returns negative on truncation (driver/ma_platform_posix.cpp:41):\n\n```c\nint _snprintf(char *buffer, size_t count, const char *format, ...) {\n    int result = vsnprintf(buffer, count, format, list);\n    if (result > count) { return count - result - 1; }   // NEGATIVE\n    return result;\n}\n```\n\n2. `MADB_PutErrorPrefix` adds the negative return to a `size_t` (driver/ma_error.cpp:155-170):\n\n```c\nerror->PrefixLen = strlen(MARIADB_ODBC_ERR_PREFIX);   // \"[ma-3.2.10]\" ~ 11\nstrcpy_s(error->SqlErrorMsg, SQL_MAX_MESSAGE_LENGTH + 1, MARIADB_ODBC_ERR_PREFIX);\nif (dbc != NULL && dbc->mariadb != NULL) {\n  error->PrefixLen += _snprintf(error->SqlErrorMsg + error->PrefixLen,\n    SQL_MAX_MESSAGE_LENGTH + 1 - error->PrefixLen,\n    \"[%s]\", mysql_get_server_info(dbc->mariadb));\n    // ^^ attacker-controlled, uncapped version string from handshake\n}\n```\n\nWith a version string of length L > ~500, the `_snprintf` return is negative, and `PrefixLen += <negative>` wraps to approximately `2^64 - (L - 510)`. The backward distance is chosen by the attacker via the version-string length.\n\n3. Two OOB write sinks use the corrupted `PrefixLen`:\n\n- `MADB_CLEAR_ERROR` / `MA_ClearError` (driver/ma_error.h:168, driver/ma_api_internal.cpp:58): `SqlErrorMsg[PrefixLen] = 0;` - one-byte OOB write\n- `MADB_SetNativeError` (driver/ma_error.cpp:231,235): `strcpy_s(SqlErrorMsg + PrefixLen, 513 - PrefixLen, msg)` - multi-byte OOB write with server-controlled error text\n\n### Steps To Reproduce\n\nEnvironment: Linux x86-64, unixODBC 2.3.14, driver built from HEAD 4e175d9 with AddressSanitizer (`cmake -DWITH_ASAN=ON`).\n\n**Step 0 - Confirm libmariadb exposes the long version** (`direct_version_check.c` attached):\n\n```bash\n$ ./direct_version_check 3402   # rogue server version_len=2000\nserver_info_len=2000  first16=AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA\n```\n\n`mysql_get_server_info()` returns the full 2000-byte handshake version uncapped.\n\n**Step 1 - Start the rogue server** (`rogue_server.py` attached):\n\n```bash\npython3 rogue_server.py 3404 4000 &   # port 3404, version string = 4000 bytes\n```\n\nThe rogue server speaks enough MariaDB wire protocol for the connection to succeed and advertises a 4000-byte `server_version` in the handshake.\n\n**Step 2 - POSITIVE (attack)** - real ASAN-built driver + real unixODBC (`odbc_client.c` attached):\n\n```bash\n./odbc_client \"DRIVER=.../build_asan/driver/libmaodbc.so;SERVER=127.0.0.1;PORT=3404;UID=u;PWD=p;SSLVERIFY=0;\"\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```bash\nSQLDriverConnect r=0                        <- connection SUCCEEDS\nallocating statement (MADB_StmtInit -> PutErrorPrefix)\n==ERROR: AddressSanitizer: heap-buffer-overflow ... WRITE of size 1\n    #0 MA_ClearError(short, void*)  driver/ma_api_internal.cpp:58\n    #1 SQLGetStmtAttr               driver/odbc_3_api.cpp:997\n    #2 libodbc.so.2                 (unixODBC Driver Manager)\n    #3 main                         odbc_client.c:32\n```\n\nThe OOB write fires at statement allocation. No application query needed.\n\n**Step 3 - NEGATIVE CONTROL** - identical path, normal version string (20 bytes):\n\n```bash\npython3 rogue_server.py 3405 20 &\n./odbc_client \"DRIVER=...;SERVER=127.0.0.1;PORT=3405;UID=u;PWD=p;SSLVERIFY=0;\"\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```bash\nSQLDriverConnect r=0\nSQLAllocHandle(STMT) r=0\nSQLExecDirect r=0 (survived)\nclean exit    EXIT=0\n```\n\nNormal-length version string causes no corruption. The long version is the sole difference.\n\n**Automated reproduction**: `bash run_poc.sh` (attached) builds the driver with ASAN, starts the rogue server, runs both positive and negative cases.\n\n### Supporting Material/References\n\n- Vulnerable function: `MADB_PutErrorPrefix` at driver/ma_error.cpp:165\n- Negative-return source: `_snprintf` at driver/ma_platform_posix.cpp:41\n- `PrefixLen` type: `size_t` at driver/ma_c_stuff.h:70\n- OOB sink 1: `MA_ClearError` at driver/ma_api_internal.cpp:58\n- OOB sink 2: `MADB_SetNativeError` at driver/ma_error.cpp:231\n- Trigger path: `MADB_StmtInit` at driver/ma_statement.cpp:4113\n- The same `_snprintf` negative-return footgun exists in `MADB_DsnToString` (Windows prompt path) and catalog-query builders - should be reviewed as part of the same fix\n- Attachments: `rogue_server.py`, `odbc_client.c`, `direct_version_check.c`, `harness.cpp`, `run_poc.sh`, `poc_evidence.txt`\n\n## Impact\n\nA malicious or MITM MariaDB/MySQL server corrupts client-process memory (out-of-bounds write) in any application using MariaDB Connector/ODBC, the moment it opens a connection and allocates a statement. The demonstrated effect is a reliable crash (denial of service) via an AddressSanitizer-confirmed heap out-of-bounds write.\n\nThe write offset is chosen by the attacker through the version-string length, and the `strcpy_s` sink (`MADB_SetNativeError`) copies attacker-influenced content (server error text) to that attacker-chosen backward offset, so corruption of adjacent heap objects - and potentially impact beyond DoS - is plausible on non-hardened builds. Only the crash is demonstrated here.\n\nBlast radius: every application (BI tools, ETL jobs, application servers, `isql`, etc.) that connects through MariaDB Connector/ODBC. Preconditions: (a) client connects to a server the attacker controls or can MITM (driver default is plaintext - no TLS), and (b) the application allocates a statement, which every ODBC workflow does. No credentials, privileges, or human interaction beyond initiating the connection are required.",
